# Usage guide

## The two tabs

- **Edit** — where you write. A list of passages on the left, an editor for the selected one on the right.
- **Play** — runs your story from the start passage, so you can test it as you go.

Switching to Play always rebuilds the story from your latest edits, so you're never testing stale text.

## Passages

A passage is one chunk of story — a scene, a page, a moment. Every passage has:

- an **ID** — a short name with no spaces, used internally to link to it (e.g. `forest`, `Chapter2Intro`)
- **text** — what the reader sees, written in the big textarea

Blank lines between lines of text start a new paragraph. There's no other formatting — no bold, italics, or headers, to keep things simple and portable.

### Creating and editing passages

- **+ New passage** — adds a new, empty passage and selects it.
- Click any passage name in the left list to open it for editing.
- Changes to the text save automatically as you type.
- Changing the **Passage ID** field renames it. Links pointing to the old name will need updating manually — see [Fixing broken links](#fixing-broken-links).
- **Delete passage** — removes the currently open passage. You can't delete the last remaining one.
- **Set as start** — marks the currently open passage as where the story begins when played. The start passage is marked `(start)` in the list.

## Links

Links are how the reader moves from one passage to another. Two forms:

```
[[Choice label->targetPassageId]]
```
Shows "Choice label" as a clickable choice; clicking it goes to `targetPassageId`.

```
[[targetPassageId]]
```
Shorthand when you want the passage ID itself as the visible label.

Example:

```
You reach a fork in the path.

[[Take the left trail->leftTrail]]
[[Take the right trail->rightTrail]]
```

Each `[[...]]` becomes one clickable choice in Play mode. You can have as many links in a passage as you want, or none at all (a dead end, or an ending).

### Fixing broken links

If you rename or delete a passage, any `[[...]]` links pointing to its old name will no longer work — in Play mode they'll show "No passage named …". The editor doesn't rewrite links for you, so after a rename, search your other passages for the old ID and update them by hand.

## Variables

Variables let a passage remember something and other passages react to it — a name, a choice made earlier, a score.

**Set a variable** (this is removed from the visible text — readers won't see it):
```
{{set:lantern=lit}}
```

**Show a variable's value:**
```
The lantern is {{get:lantern}}.
```

**Branch on a variable is done using two passages, one that checks and writes**, since Sapphire Novel Engine keeps its syntax deliberately small. A common pattern is to set a variable when the reader makes a choice, and simply route them to a different passage depending on that choice — the routing *is* the branching:

```
start:
  You see a locked door.
  [[Search for a key->search]]
  [[Force it open->force]]

search:
  {{set:method=search}}
  You find a key under the mat.
  [[Open the door->reveal]]

force:
  {{set:method=force}}
  You kick it in. That'll leave a mark.
  [[Go through->reveal]]

reveal:
  However you got in ({{get:method}}), the room is empty.
```

Variables persist for the rest of the playthrough and reset when the reader (or you, in Play mode) clicks **restart**.

## Testing your story

Switch to the **Play** tab any time. At the bottom of each passage you'll see:
- which passage you're currently on
- the current value of every variable set so far

This is there to help you debug — it won't appear in the exported story.

Click **↺ restart** to reset variables and jump back to the start passage.

## Exporting

When your story is ready to share, click **Export standalone HTML** (on the Edit tab). This downloads a `story.html` file containing:

- just your passages and variables — no editor UI
- the same link and variable behavior as Play mode
- a restart button

This exported file is what you'd post publicly, email, or upload to a game page — it's read-only and has no editing tools, just the finished story.

## Tips

- Keep passage IDs short and consistent (`forest_entrance`, not `The Entrance To The Dark Forest`) — you'll be typing them into links often.
- Use **Set as start** early, so Play mode always opens where you expect.
- Test with **Play** frequently rather than writing many passages before checking any links — broken links are easiest to catch right after you write them.
- There's no autosave to disk — your story only lives in the browser tab until you **Export**. Export often, especially before closing the tab.
